So you can also view this resistance as sort of like a tendency to play with the source text, to be like, okay, I talk about co-authoring it along with the author. So there's always a degree that happens. No two people ever read the same book. You know, you know that very well as an author. And there's some stuff in the psychology of reading, especially in the educational literature, that focuses on this and how we put stuff into the books we read. You read it through your own experiences.
所以你也可以将这种抵抗视为一种与源文本玩耍的倾向，比如，好吧，我谈论的是与作者共同创作。所以总会有一定程度的这种情况发生。从来没有哪两个人读过同一本书。你知道的，作为一个作家，你非常清楚这一点。阅读心理学中有些内容，尤其是在教育文献中，侧重于这一点，以及我们是如何将各种东西融入我们所读的书中的。你是通过自己的经历去阅读的。

60
00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:28.000
You fill in gaps, all of these kinds of different things. And so I was really interested in, well, what are the things that fans are doing? So you give them a gap. They start filling it. You're not sure what's happening. You're going to theorize about what's happening next. Something you don't like happens. you're going to do this.
你填补空白，所有这些不同类型的事情。所以我真的很感兴趣的是，粉丝们都在做些什么？你给他们一个空白。他们开始填补。你不确定接下来会发生什么。你会开始推测接下来会发生什么。发生了你不喜欢的事情。你就会这么做。

61
00:34:28.000 --> 00:35:01.000
And so I started making lists of, well, I want to give readers plenty of opportunities in the book to do this. So it's having the big decisions where they wish something different had happened. It's baiting gaps. And sometimes it's really simple stuff. So like in the inheritance games, the risk-taking dangerous competition guy has this giant scar down his torso. And you just don't know how he got it for multiple books.
所以我开始列清单：嗯，我想在书中给读者提供充足的机会来做这件事。比如设置重大的抉择，让他们希望当时能有不同的事情发生。比如制造空白。有时是非常简单的事情。比如在《继承游戏》中，那个爱冒险、危险的竞争者男主角的躯干上有一道巨大的伤疤。有好几本书里，你都不知道他是怎么弄到这道伤疤的。

62
00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:20.000
And that's a gap that you're actually leaving there for the audience to fill. Why? Well, then the fans can go out and talk about like, how do you think Jameson got that scar? This is how I think he got that scar. I have a friend who wrote a book once and they were always referring to like that thing that happened in Peru. And you're like, what happened in Peru?
而这就是你实际上留给读者去填补的空白。为什么？因为这样粉丝们就可以出去讨论：你觉得杰姆森是怎么弄到这个伤疤的？我是这么觉得的。我有一个朋友曾经写过一本书，他们总是提到“发生在秘鲁的那件事”。然后你会问：秘鲁发生什么了？

63
00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:38.000
And they'd respond with random things like, we all agreed. That monkey was perfectly trained at the time. I always think about that. It's my friend Ali Carter wrote a book. And you're like, what happened in Peru with the monkey and the this and the that? So it's baiting that. It's like where you cut a chapter. Like are you cutting it?
他们会随机回答一些事情，比如：我们当时都同意了。那只猴子当时训练得非常完美。我总是想起这件事。我的朋友阿里·卡特写过一本书。然后你会想：秘鲁到底发生了什么，怎么还有猴子和这啊那啊的？所以这就是在制造悬念。就像你在哪里切断一章节。就像你在切断它吗？

64
00:35:38.000 --> 00:36:15.000
And then like sometimes I remember in, I think it was book three of inheritance games. There was this moment where the two brothers go off. And Avery, the main character, goes home. And the brothers are kind of at both in a weird emotional spot and everything. And a few hours later, they come home and they're like, caked in mud, and one of them is bleeding. And just in the entire series, you never find out what happened in those hours, because that is something I'm reading, leaving for the readers to kind of find out there.
然后有时候我记得在，我想是《继承游戏》第三本里。有这样一个时刻，两个兄弟出去了。艾薇，也就是主角，回家了。兄弟俩在情绪上都处于一种奇怪的状态等等。几个小时后，他们回家了，身上沾满了泥，其中一个人还在流血。在整部系列中，你都从未得知在这几个小时里到底发生了什么，因为那是留给读者自己去发掘的东西。

65
00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:39.000
I'm also really interested in I'll look at specific fan activities. So something like cosplay, where they will actually create versions of the costumes and dress up of the characters. And you can lose hours to looking at cosplay because there are absolute artists who do incredible, incredible cosplays. So, you know, one of the items on my little workbook is, like, does the book pass the cosplay test?
我也对特定的粉丝活动很感兴趣。比如角色扮演，他们会实际制作服装的版本并打扮成角色。你会花上几个小时来欣赏角色扮演，因为有绝对的艺术家做出了令人难以置信的角色扮演。所以，你知道，我的小工作簿上的项目之一就是，这本书经得起角色扮演的考验吗？

66
00:36:39.000 --> 00:37:01.000
Like, the low level of it is the Halloween test. So, like, can a 13-year-old dress up as someone from this book for Halloween? How would they do that? What does it look like? So you think of something like Harry Potter, for instance, and it's actually really easy to dress up. up as Harry Potter, even if you don't have the official gear. So even back before they had all the
比如，低级别的测试就是万圣节测试。所以，13岁的孩子能为万圣节打扮成这本书里的人吗？他们会怎么做？它看起来是什么样的？所以你会想到比如《哈利·波特》这样的作品，实际上打扮成哈利·波特真的很容易，即使你没有官方的装备。所以甚至在他们拥有所有那些

67
00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:53.000
tie-in stuff, like you drew a lightning-shaped scar on your forehead, and you wore a black cloak, and you carried a stick and wore glasses, and you were Harry Potter. So anyone could throw together a costume and be Harry Potter and recognizably Harry Potter very easily. So that's a question I asked myself, like, is this a book? Like, who could they dress up as? How could they dress up as that person? Like if someone wanted to throw a theme party based on the book, I have a test, I call the theme party test. I'm like, okay, how's the book given them like a format for the invitation? So, like, I have a book coming out in at the end of July called The Grandest Game, which is a new series set in the universe of the inheritance games where it's all these people coming to win like $30 million or something very big by solving all these puzzles and riddles and games.
衍生周边之前，比如你在额头上画一个闪电形状的伤疤，穿着一件黑斗篷，拿着一根棍子，戴着眼镜，你就是哈利·波特。所以任何人都可以随手拼凑出一套服装，成为哈利·波特，而且非常容易被认出是哈利·波特。所以这是我问自己的一个问题，比如，这是一本书吗？读者可以打扮成谁？他们怎么打扮成那个人？就像如果有人想根据这本书举办一个主题派对，我有一个测试，我称之为主题派对测试。我想，好吧，这本书有没有给他们提供邀请函的格式？所以，比如，我有一本将于7月底出版的书，叫做《最伟大的游戏》，这是一部设定在《继承游戏》宇宙中的新系列，讲的是所有人来赢得大约3000万美元或非常大的一笔奖金，通过解开所有这些谜题、谜语和游戏。

68
00:37:53.000 --> 00:38:18.000
And I'm like, well, to pass the theme party test, ideally there'd be like a really elaborate invitation you get to play the game. So there are these like Willie Wonka-style golden tickets. And I'm like, well, if you want to throw a grandest game party, here's your ticket. And the question is, like, what are the, what's the drink that's in the theme party test? Or what's the food? Or what do people dress us? So there's a masquerade ball so people can dress up as the characters.
我想，为了通过主题派对测试，理想情况下会有一份非常精致的邀请函供你玩这个游戏。所以会有威利·旺卡风格的金门票。我想，如果你想办一个最伟大的游戏派对，这就是你的门票。问题是，主题派对里的饮料是什么？或者食物是什么？或者人们穿什么衣服？所以会有一个化妆舞会，这样人们就可以打扮成角色。

69
00:38:18.000 --> 00:39:03.000
So I kind of go through and look at a variety of these different things. in terms of the activities, in terms of what I think is going on with the psychology. And then I've also done a related amount of work that was sort of more inductive work that I did for myself on. I'd look at, okay, what are the 20 TV shows that have the most fan fiction written for them? And can I extract sort of qualitatively what those shows have in common? And so people think of fandom as being a very sci-fi and fantasy-driven thing, but the 20 shows were actually, there was no difference between sci-fi and fantasy and realistic shows. So that didn't come out statistically significant. But what did come out as
所以我大致浏览并研究了各种这些不同的事情，就活动而言，就我所认为的心理学运作机制而言。然后我还做了相应数量的工作，这更像是我自己做的归纳工作。我研究了，好吧，同人小说最多的20个电视节目是什么？我能否从定性上提取出这些节目共同拥有的东西？所以人们认为粉丝圈是一个非常偏向科幻和奇幻的事物，但这20个节目实际上，科幻奇幻和现实主义节目之间没有任何区别。所以在统计学上并不显著。但统计学上显著的是

70
00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:56.000
statistically significant was the predominance of procedural shows. So medical procedures, police procedurals, or sci-fi and fantasy that had a procedural element. So you think of something like Star Trek or supernatural, right? So those are sci-fi and fantasy. They also have a very strong procedural setup. I think once upon a time was on there, Gray's Anatomy. And so then I asked myself, well, why do I think procedural are so good at this? And I think part of the reason is that the procedural element, so the case of the week essentially takes up enough space in the narrative. So monster hunting and supernatural, the surgeries in Grey's Anatomy. And those things take up enough space that you kind of ration out some of the really juicy character moments a little more.
程序剧的统治地位。比如医疗程序剧、警察程序剧，或者带有程序剧元素的科幻奇幻剧。所以你会想到像《星际迷航》或《邪恶力量》这样的作品，对吧？所以那些是科幻奇幻剧。它们也有非常强的程序剧设定。我想《童话镇》也在其中，《实习医生格蕾》也是。然后我问自己，为什么我认为程序剧在这方面这么厉害？我想部分原因在于程序剧元素，也就是基本上每周一个的案件占据了叙事中的足够空间。所以《邪恶力量》中的怪物狩猎，《实习医生格蕾》中的外科手术。这些事情占用了足够的空间，所以你会把一些真正精彩的角色时刻稍微多分配、拉长一点。

71
00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:35.000
And a lot of what people do is they're like, ooh, I want more of that. I once heard that I worked for a while in a project with one of the guys who was one of the big guys behind criminal minds. And he used to kind of call those cookies where it's the moment where they really want to see these two characters together. but it's not a show where you get to see them together all the time. And so I have a list of about 10 properties that seem to be fandom enabling. There are things like the stories tend to be temporally expansive and expand, usually into the past, though sometimes into the future in significant ways. They're emotionally expansive, meaning they, you're going to have really funny parts
人们做的很多事情都是，哦，我想要更多那样的情节。我曾经听说——我曾在一个项目中与《犯罪心理》幕后的一位大佬合作过一段时间。他过去常常把那些时刻称为“饼干”，也就是他们真的想看到这两个角色在一起的时刻，但这不是一个你能一直看到他们在一起的节目。所以我的清单上有大约10个似乎具有粉丝圈催化作用的属性。它们包括：故事往往在时间上具有扩张性，并且会扩展，通常是向过去扩展，尽管有时也会以显著的方式向未来扩展。它们在情感上具有扩张性，这意味着你会经历非常搞笑的部分

72
00:40:35.000 --> 00:41:08.000
and really sad parts. It's fluff, it's angst, it's all of these different things at once. So it'll be like Buffy the Vampire Slayer. There are some episodes that were just really funny episodes. And then you have like an episode where Buffy discovers her mom's body that's just like an exploration of grief and devastating. So the things that tend to provoke fandom tend to have the whole spectrum. They're not just funny. If they're sitcoms, they're sitcoms that actually do have their sad episodes a little bit more.
和非常悲伤的部分。既有轻松甜蜜（fluff），又有虐心痛苦（angst），所有这些不同的东西同时存在。所以它会像《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》一样。有些集数真的非常搞笑。然后你会有这样一集，巴菲发现了她妈妈的遗体，这就纯粹是对悲痛的探索，极具毁灭性。所以倾向于激发出粉丝圈的事物往往涵盖了整个光谱。它们不仅仅是搞笑。如果是情景喜剧，那也是那些确实会更多地上演悲伤剧集的喜剧。

73
00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:50.000
They tend to have a ton, a ton of love stories. in them, both romantic love stories, but also platonic love stories, like a brotherly love story or a friendship love story. I was thinking in Gray's Anatomy there I was talking about, that's my person, like Christina and Meredith had this friendship, and they were persons. You know, they were each other's person. And so it's the things that to be fandom provoking have all of these love stories of all kinds to give you all these relationships to get invested with. So, you know, that stuff that I left academia before I could go into a lab and test all of this in a kind of official way. But I had the theories and I had the predictions.
它们往往包含大量的爱情故事，既有浪漫的爱情故事，也有柏拉图式的爱情故事，比如兄弟之爱或友谊之爱。我当时在想《实习医生格蕾》里我提到过的，那是我的“人”，就像克里斯蒂娜和梅雷迪思有这种友谊，她们是彼此的“人”。你知道，她们是彼此的“人”。所以那些倾向于激发粉丝圈的事物，拥有所有这些各种各样的爱情故事，为你提供所有这些可以投入情感的关系。所以，你知道，在我离开学术界进入实验室以某种官方方式测试所有这些之前，我就已经做了这些事情。但我有理论，也有预测。

74
00:41:50.000 --> 00:42:32.000
And so I'm able to much more informally and non-scientifically test them in my writing where I'm like, okay, what if I do all of this stuff? Both this stuff about how do you like appeal to a broad audience because you're doing how the brain is kind of really focused on stories and what kind of stories we like. and also all the fandomy stuff. And so Inheritance Games was really the first book I ever wrote where I synthesized all of that and tried to do all of that on both the fandom side of the equation and the broadly appealing side of the equation. It was just really, really deliberate about saying what happens if I do all of this in one book?
所以我能够在我自己的写作中以更非正式、非科学的方式来测试它们，我想：好吧，如果我把所有这些东西都加进去会怎么样？既有关于如何吸引广大受众的东西，因为你在处理大脑如何真正专注于故事以及我们喜欢什么样的故事，也有所有粉丝圈的元素。所以《继承游戏》真的是我写的标志着我综合了所有这些的第一本书，并试图在粉丝圈的一侧和广泛吸引力的另一侧同时做到这两点。我是非常、非常刻意地在思考：如果我把所有这些都放在一本书里会发生什么？

75
00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:54.000
And it was my 21st book. So, of course, it's not just this is the only thing in the book. It has the structure. It has the character arcs. It has the plot. It has all the craft I've developed over, you know, more than 20 books. But it also has all of this stuff added in on top. And what happened is as far as experiments go, it was beyond my wildest dreams of what an experiment would look like.
而且这是我的第21本书。所以，当然，它不仅包含这些。它有结构。它有人物弧光。它有情节。它拥有我通过（你知道的）20多本书所磨练出的所有写作技艺。但它还在顶部添加了所有这些额外的内容。结果就是，就实验而言，它超出了我所能想象的最疯狂的实验梦想。

76
00:42:54.000 --> 00:43:27.000
Because, again, I had published 20 previous books. And I kind of had an idea of what it looked like when I published a book. I had never hit a bestseller list. I had never really had a huge fandom. I was kind of what they call in publishing a mid-list author. So I was able to have a career. I was publishing one or two books almost every year from the time I was very young until now. But then I put all of the science in this book and I wrote the inheritance games.
因为，再次说明，我已经出版了20本书。我对出版一本书时的情形大致心里有数。我从未登上过畅销书榜。我从未真正拥有过一个庞大的粉丝圈。我在出版界被称为“中档作者”。所以我能够维持一份职业生涯。从我很年轻的时候直到现在，我几乎每年都会出版一本书或两本书。但后来我把所有这些科学原理融入了这本书，我写了《继承游戏》。

77
00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:56.000
And suddenly it did hit the bestseller list and spent, you know, as a series, it spent more than 80 weeks on the series list up there with like, you know, HagerGaves and Harry Potter and Winpy Kid and Percy Jackson and like just even being on a list with all of those series. I was like, what is happening there? But I really do, you know, there's a lot of, in any publishing thing, there's a lot of luck involved and timing. Then you have to have a brilliant publishing team who's making the right packaging decisions.
然后它突然真的上了畅销书榜，并且作为系列，它在系列榜上停留了80多周，和《饥饿游戏》、《哈利·波特》、《小屁孩日记》、《波西·杰克逊》等作品并列，甚至能和所有这些系列一起上榜，我都觉得：这到底是怎么回事？但我真的认为，你知道，在任何出版事务中，都有很多运气和时机的成分。然后你必须拥有一个出色的出版团队，他们做出正确的包装决策。

78
00:43:56.000 --> 00:44:25.000
So there are, of course, many other variables at play. But in terms of what I did differently, this book, It really did boil down to the science of it. Well, it's just amazing how you've really applied what you know as a psychologist to becoming the writer you are today. And it's very, very impressive. So I just want to ask one question, which is you've left academia. But what do you think are the most interesting questions out there for psychologists who are still looking at the psychology of fiction? That is a great question.
所以，当然，还有许多其他变量在起作用。但就我在这本书中所做的不同之处而言，它确实归结为科学原理。嗯，你真的把身为人心理学家的知识应用到了成为今天的作家的道路上，这真是太令人惊叹了，非常、非常深刻。所以我只想问一个问题，那就是你已经离开了学术界。但对于那些仍在研究虚构作品心理学的心理学家来说，你认为目前最有趣的问题是什么？这是一个很好的问题。

79
00:44:25.000 --> 00:45:08.000
And I have a huge list somewhere of the things. You know, it hits me on a daily basis where I'm like, oh, I want. wonder. And back when I had my lab, anytime I suddenly started wondering about some of that, I could just go in a lab and I could develop the study and I could test it. And then oftentimes I was wrong about what I thought would be true and I'd discover something else. I think there is a big gap. So the area I've been playing in theoretically and testing in my writing in terms of what people like in stories, I think that's actually compared to some other areas understudy. currently. So again, I have all these theories. I put it in my writing. I really only ever did
我脑海中某处有一个长长的清单。你知道，它每天都会击中我，让我觉得：哦，我想知道。以前当我还在实验室时，每当我突然开始对其中的某些事情感到好奇时，我就可以直接走进实验室，开发一项研究并对它进行测试。然后很多时候，我对什么是真的这一设想其实是错的，而我会发现其他的东西。我认为这里有一个巨大的鸿沟。所以我一直在理论上探讨并在写作中测试的领域，即人们在故事中喜欢什么，与一些其他领域相比，我认为目前其实研究不足。所以再次强调，我有所有的这些理论。我把它写进我的作品里。我实际上只做过

80
00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:52.000
one study that actually tested empirically a lot of these theories. I did a study on book titles and whether or not the words were buzzwords created by one of the theories of why we like fiction versus whether they were like a controlled word. And we did find in that study that the titles that were based on, I think, four or five different theories about why we liked fiction. All of those theories were generating titles that people were more interested in reading the book than we actually used words that had already appeared on the New York Times list. So words that should have been compelling, but they outperformed all of those. But that was the one study we did on that.
一项真正以实证方式测试其中许多理论的研究。我做过一项关于书名的研究，看看这些词是否是根据我们为什么喜欢小说的某种理论所创造的流行语，还是说它们就像受控词汇。我们在研究中确实发现，那些基于（我想是四到五个关于为什么我们喜欢小说的不同理论）的书名。所有这些理论产生的书名，都比我们实际上使用已经出现在《纽约时报》榜单上的词汇更能激起读者阅读该书的兴趣。换句话说，那些本该很有吸引力的词汇，被它们给比下去了。但那是我们在这方面做的唯一一项研究。

81
00:45:52.000 --> 00:46:41.000
And I think it would be a really fascinating area for people to look into because it really does seem to be an area where it's not like there's just one theory that explains it all. It really does seem, at least from that first experiment we did, like it wasn't like only one theory predicted good words. We tested multiple different theories. So we tested the gossip theory, the pleasure theory, theory of mind theory. We did one on morality. And like all of those different domains were actually outperforming controls. So it seems like it might be an area where the, big picture of why we like stories may lend some support to a bunch of different theories, kind of all operating at the same time, which could then also be very interesting in terms
我认为这对于人们来说将是一个非常迷人的研究领域，因为它似乎确实是一个这样的领域：并不是只有一个理论能解释一切。至少从我们做的第一个实验来看，似乎并不存在只有单一理论能预测出好词的情况。我们测试了多种不同的理论。所以我们测试了八卦理论、愉悦理论、心智理论。我们还做了一项关于道德的研究。所有这些不同的领域实际上都表现得比对照组更好。所以这似乎是一个这样的领域：我们为什么喜欢故事的宏观图景可能会为一系列不同的理论提供某种支持，这些理论似乎都在同时运作，这反过来对于

82
00:46:41.000 --> 00:47:20.000
of those theories themselves. So, like, is there a synthesis of a lot of these theories where it's not just, is it this or is it this? But maybe it's some combination of those things. And maybe the reason that we like stories so much is that it's this synthesis of a lot of different things coming together to kind of make this perfect storm. And then I would also say the psychology of fandom, again, where you're actually testing. Before I left, one of my graduate students was doing a study on cosplay, where you'd actually like come in and you'd dress up as either a hero or a villain. And then we were testing a bunch of things, but embedded in those are moral things to see,
这些理论本身也非常有趣。所以，比如，是否存在对其中许多理论的综合，即不仅仅是“是这个还是那个”？也许它是这些东西的某种结合。也许我们之所以如此喜欢故事，是因为它是许多不同事物结合在一起，共同形成了这场完美的风暴。然后我还要说粉丝圈的心理学，同样地，你需要去实际测试它。在我离开之前，我的一个研究生正在做一项关于角色扮演的研究，你会真正来到实验室，打扮成英雄或反派。然后我们测试了一堆东西，但其中嵌入了道德相关的东西，以便观察：

83
00:47:20.000 --> 00:48:10.000
like, does your moral perspective change if you're wearing a Voldemort costume instead of a Harry Potter costume. I had a student when I left who was looking at daydreaming about fictional characters, which some of those studies have, there's some fascinating stuff that's been done out there on that, but pushing it further. And there are, when I was last in academia, there were, we were starting to see work. A lot of it was coming from graduate students. As like there, we saw, I was seeing a lot of stuff that was a master's thesis or, you know, an upcoming PhD student who was looking at actually testing some of these things about the psychology of fandom. I think oftentimes it's people who are either involved in fandom themselves or at least
如果你穿着伏地魔的服装而不是哈利·波特的服装，你的道德视角会改变吗？我离开时有一个学生正在研究对虚构角色的白日梦，关于这方面已经有一些迷人的研究成果被做出来了，但还要将它进一步推进。而且，自我最后一次身处学术界时起，我们开始看到相关研究的出现。其中很多都来自研究生。正如我们所看到的，我看到了很多硕士论文或即将毕业的博士生所做的研究，他们实际上在着手测试关于粉丝圈心理学的一些事情。我认为通常这些人要么自己就是粉丝圈的参与者，或者至少

84
00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:54.000
know the ins and outs of it really well. And they were coming up with some really fascinating stuff. So I think in the years to come, I'm really interested in sort of seeing that generation of students taking off to really see sort of this field that was really, in some ways at its beginning, really start to flourish once these people who were driving it, who were so young and early in their careers, advance a little more. Well, Dr. Barnes, I want to thank you for joining me today. This has been a lot of fun and learned a lot about writing fiction. And now it's time for me to go work on my book. Well, thank you so much for having me. Like I said, I love talking about psychology, so this was an absolute class.
对里面的门道非常了解。他们提出了一些非常迷人的东西。所以我认为在未来的几年里，我真的很有兴趣看到那一代理学生起飞，真正看到这个在某些方面还处于起步阶段的领域开始蓬勃发展，一旦这些推动它前进的、如此年轻且职业生涯初期的年轻人再取得一些进展。好的，巴恩斯博士，我要感谢你今天能来到这里。这非常有趣，我也学到了很多关于写小说的知识。现在是时候让我去写我的书了。非常感谢邀请我。就像我说的，我喜欢谈论心理学，所以这绝对是一场精彩的体验。

